\section{Controlled Evaluation Subset}
\label{sec:fair_subset}

The 1.7B evidence tax comparison (\S\ref{sec:scaling}) is confounded by differential truncation rates. At \texttt{max\_new\_tokens}$=$1024, the no-evidence model truncates 50.9\% of outputs (501/985 documents) versus 31.9\% (314/985) for the evidence model. To address this, we adopt a matched@2048 fair evaluation: both configurations are evaluated at \texttt{max\_new\_tokens}$=$2048, and the tax is computed on the intersection of documents where both produce format-compliant output. This yields 971/985 fair-subset documents, with a tax of $-$5.63pp (s42), confirming a genuine 1.7B evidence benefit. As additional validation, a stricter controlled subset at @1024 (excluding truncated or repetitive outputs; 442/985 documents) yields $-$6.2pp, with the stronger benefit attributable to the more aggressive filtering.

Table~\ref{tab:mechanistic} provides a mechanistic breakdown across three scales (evaluated at @1024). Output duplication rate (fraction of documents containing $\geq$3 repeated triples or unique/total $< 0.5$) declines monotonically with scale for both training configurations, but the gap between no-evidence and with-evidence models narrows from 27.5pp at 1.7B to 3.7pp at 8B. Two mechanisms may explain this pattern. The parsimonious account is a structural output constraint: evidence formatting occupies token budget, leaving fewer tokens for triples, which reduces false positives and duplicates---a purely output-level effect. However, under matched@2048 evaluation where truncation is controlled, the 1.7B evidence benefit persists ($-$5.63pp), partially challenging a pure output-constraint explanation and suggesting a possible representation-level effect where evidence generation encourages the model to attend to supporting context. Our data cannot distinguish the relative contributions of these two mechanisms; controlled experiments with fixed output length but varied evidence requirements would be needed. At 4B, duplication is already moderate (31.1\% with evidence), so either mechanism's benefit diminishes; under matched@2048 evaluation, the 4B training-time tax is approximately neutral (+0.25$\pm$0.47pp, three-seed mean). The truncation asymmetry reverses across scales: at 1.7B the no-evidence model truncates 19pp more often; at 8B the evidence model truncates 9pp more often due to the longer output format.

\begin{table}[t]

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We evaluate on DocRED~\citep{yao2019docred} (3,053 training / 998 dev documents, 96 relation types) with Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred} for cross-dataset validation. Our primary model is Qwen3-4B; we also train Qwen3-1.7B, 8B, and 14B to study scale effects, with LLaMA-3.1-8B for cross-family validation. All models use LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} ($r{=}64$, $\alpha{=}128$) with structured output jointly producing triples and evidence. RSFT selects the top-$k{=}1$ of $N{=}8$ samples by CED score (\S\ref{sec:ced}); GRPO uses group sizes $G{=}4$ (QLoRA) and $G{=}8$ (bf16); DPO constructs preference pairs from CED-ranked outputs. We report Ign-F1 (primary; ignores training-set triples), Rel-F1, Evi-F1 (TP-only and joint per DREEAM protocol~\citep{ma2023dreeam}), and EDCR (\S\ref{sec:edcr}). Detailed hyperparameters appear in the Appendix.


\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the primary comparison on Qwen3-4B. The SFT baseline exhibits high seed sensitivity (mean Ign-F1 43.58, std 3.80pp, range 39.74--47.34). Removing evidence entirely (no-evidence SFT, s42) reaches 47.21 Ign-F1.

RSFT CED achieves 44.82 Ign-F1 (+1.24 over SFT mean) with substantially lower variance (std 0.51pp), though these reflect different sources of variation (Section~\ref{sec:scaling}). Even the weakest RSFT seed (44.23) exceeds the SFT mean. Evidence quality improves (Evi-F1 80.96 vs.\ 79.61) and coupling tightens (EDCR 0.6487 vs.\ 0.6797). Per-seed results appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:ablation_full}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Main results on DocRED dev (Qwen3-4B). Ign-F1 shows mean $\pm$ std$^{(\text{seeds})}$; other metrics are same-seed means. Best \textbf{bold}; best evidence-generating \underline{underlined}. Per-seed: Appendix~\ref{tab:ablation_full}.}
\label{tab:main}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Ign-F1} & \textbf{Rel-F1} & \textbf{Evi-F1} & \textbf{EDCR} \\
\midrule
SFT baseline & 43.58{\scriptsize$\pm$3.80}$^{(3)}$ & 44.39 & 79.61 & 0.6797 \\
SFT no-evidence & \textbf{47.21} & \textbf{48.76} & -- & -- \\
RSFT CED & \underline{44.82{\scriptsize$\pm$0.51}$^{(3)}$} & \underline{45.80} & 80.96 & 0.6487 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}%
}
\vspace{-2mm}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_scaling.pdf}
\caption{Evidence tax and RSFT gain across model scales. The gap between no-evidence and SFT bars reveals the scale-dependent evidence tax; RSFT consistently narrows this gap.}
\label{fig:scaling}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Evidence-Extraction Decoupling Spectrum}
\label{sec:spectrum}

Because evidence and extraction share gradient capacity, different optimization paradigms handle this competition in qualitatively different ways. We organize the outcomes along a \emph{decoupling spectrum} from catastrophic collapse to no decoupling (Figure~\ref{fig:spectrum}; full data in Table~\ref{tab:spectrum}, Appendix). Figure~\ref{fig:grpo_dynamics} (Appendix) traces these dynamics across training checkpoints.

\paragraph{GRPO.}
Under QLoRA 4-bit quantization ($G{=}4$), GRPO drives Rel-F1 to 1.38 while Evi-F1 rises to 85.04 (EDCR 0.9876): quantization-constrained precision limits the effective parameter space, so the reward signal preferentially optimizes the easier sub-task~\citep{gao2023scaling}, resembling the quantized-actor divergence of \citet{li2026qurl}. Full-precision bf16 ($G{=}8$) avoids catastrophe (Rel-F1 45.49, EDCR 0.6318). The two configurations differ in quantization precision, group size ($G{=}4$ vs.\ $8$), and effective batch size (QLoRA: $4{\times}16{=}64$ documents vs.\ bf16: $8{\times}4{=}32$); quantization is the most likely primary factor, but these confounds cannot be fully disentangled from the current experiments.

\paragraph{DPO and RSFT.}
DPO exhibits substantial extraction degradation: Rel-F1 drops from 33.37 (1 epoch) to 31.45 (3 epochs) while Evi-F1 rises (80.90$\to$84.84). EDCR is non-monotonic (0.8123$\to$0.7503): despite worsening extraction, evidence selectivity improves with continued training, partially reducing the decoupling. RSFT maintains both capabilities simultaneously (Rel-F1 45.80, Evi-F1 80.96, EDCR 0.6487) because discrete output selection cannot exploit the extraction-evidence trade-off.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_spectrum.pdf}
\caption{Decoupling spectrum. Each method is plotted by Rel-F1 (x-axis) and Evi-F1 (y-axis). Arrows indicate training progression for DPO and GRPO bf16. Decoupled methods drift toward the upper-left.}
\label{fig:spectrum}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Scale-Dependent Evidence Tax}
\label{sec:scaling}

The evidence tax---Ign-F1(no-evidence) minus Ign-F1(with-evidence), so positive values indicate cost and negative values indicate benefit---is scale-dependent. Under matched evaluation (\texttt{max\_new\_tokens}$=$2048, format-compliant intersection; Appendix~\ref{sec:training_tax}), the training-time tax reveals a within-family capacity threshold: evidence generation benefits 1.7B ($-$5.63pp), where it also halves the output duplication rate (60.5\%$\to$33.0\%; Table~\ref{tab:mechanistic} in Appendix~\ref{sec:fair_subset}), but is approximately neutral at $\geq$4B (+0.25$\pm$0.47pp at 4B, $n{=}3$; +0.04pp at 8B; +0.03pp at 14B). The 1.7B and 8B no-evidence baselines use a single seed (s42); the 1.7B s42 is the strongest SFT seed (+3.22pp above the three-seed mean), so the 1.7B benefit may be amplified relative to the population mean. Table~\ref{tab:scaling} and Figure~\ref{fig:evidence_tax} present RSFT performance and inference-time evidence tax across five models (Figure~\ref{fig:scaling}).

RSFT navigates this capacity landscape through quality-based data selection rather than gradient pressure. Mean Ign-F1 improves across tested scales: +6.55pp at 1.7B, +1.24pp at 4B, +4.14pp at 8B, and +3.44pp at 14B (Table~\ref{tab:scaling}).

We observe lower cross-seed variance under RSFT at all four tested configurations (SFT std $>$ RSFT std; Appendix~\ref{sec:variance_ci}), though the effect reaches statistical significance only at 1.7B (non-overlapping $\chi^2$ CIs). Each RSFT seed starts from its corresponding independently trained SFT checkpoint (\S\ref{sec:paradigms}), so the variance reduction reflects data quality filtering rather than shared initialization. Nevertheless, interpretation is limited by $n{=}3$ seeds and the two-stage nature of RSFT (SFT then filtered retraining) versus single-stage SFT. The 1.7B RSFT model (51.19 Ign-F1, std 0.09pp) approaches 8B SFT performance (50.19, std 3.23pp) at 4.7$\times$ fewer parameters.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{RSFT performance and inference-time evidence tax across model scales (DocRED dev Ign-F1). LLaMA-3.1-8B included for cross-family validation. Mean $\pm$ std$^{(\text{seeds})}$.}
\label{tab:scaling}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{SFT w/ evi} & \textbf{RSFT} & \textbf{RSFT Tax}$^\dagger$ & \textbf{RSFT Gain} \\
\midrule
Qwen3-1.7B & 44.64{\scriptsize$\pm$2.79}$^{(3)}$ & 51.19{\scriptsize$\pm$0.09}$^{(3)}$ & $-$7.75 & +6.55 \\
Qwen3-4B & 43.58{\scriptsize$\pm$3.80}$^{(3)}$ & 44.82{\scriptsize$\pm$0.51}$^{(3)}$ & +0.94 & +1.24 \\
Qwen3-8B & 50.19{\scriptsize$\pm$3.23}$^{(3)}$ & 54.33{\scriptsize$\pm$0.50}$^{(3)}$ & $-$0.78 & +4.14 \\
Qwen3-14B & 55.07$^{(1)}$ & 58.51{\scriptsize$\pm$0.50}$^{(2)}$ & $-$2.78{\scriptsize$\pm$2.49}$^{(2)}$ & +3.44 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & 51.09{\scriptsize$\pm$2.92}$^{(3)}$ & 56.54{\scriptsize$\pm$0.64}$^{(3)}$ & $-$2.17{\scriptsize$\pm$0.76}$^{(3)}$ & +5.45 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}%
}
\vspace{1mm}
{\raggedright\footnotesize $^\dagger$Inference-time: same RSFT model with/without evidence. No-evi uses @2048; with-evi uses @1024 (1.7B: @2048). Tax $=$ no-evi $-$ with-evi; positive $=$ cost. Training-time tax in Appendix~\ref{sec:training_tax}.\par}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_evidence_tax.pdf}
\vspace{-2mm}
\caption{Inference-time evidence tax across model scales (RSFT models, Re-DocRED test). Positive $=$ cost, negative $=$ benefit. Error bars: $\pm$1 std (all scales $\geq$2 seeds).}
\label{fig:evidence_tax}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Evidence Strategy Comparison}
\label{sec:evidence_strategy}

We compare joint generation against post-hoc BM25 retrieval (entity-constrained, $k{=}2$, matching the median gold evidence count per triple in DocRED), using Evi-F1 (joint) with the DREEAM-protocol denominator~\citep{ma2023dreeam}. Post-hoc retrieval achieves higher Rel-F1 (48.76) by avoiding the evidence tax but lower Evi-F1 (joint) of 34.7 (EDCR 0.766). RSFT CED reaches 36.1 (EDCR 0.6487), a 1.4-point evidence advantage. CED reranking at inference (best-of-$N{=}8$ by CED score) achieves Rel-F1 48.64 and Evi-F1 81.88 (TP-only), approaching no-evidence extraction quality while preserving evidence.


\subsection{Generalization}
\label{sec:generalization}

Cross-architecture validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B (Table~\ref{tab:scaling}) shows the largest RSFT gain (+5.45pp) with complete pairwise seed separation (\S\ref{sec:scaling}). The RSFT improvement transfers across datasets: on Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred}, RSFT achieves 41.45 Ign-F1 (+4.86pp over SFT 36.59), matching the DocRED gain (+5.08pp). Our best joint Evi-F1 (36.1, DREEAM-protocol) falls below supervised DREEAM (54.15\%~\citep{ma2023dreeam}), a gap driven by extraction performance rather than evidence quality (TP-only Evi-F1 exceeds 80\%). On the Re-DocRED test set, RSFT Ign-F1 drops approximately 20pp relative to dev across all evaluated scales (e.g., 14B: 58.5$\to$38.5), attributable to 2.82$\times$ higher annotation density in test (34.7 vs.\ 12.3 gold triples per document); model ranking is perfectly preserved (Spearman $\rho{=}1.0$; Table~\ref{tab:test_results} in Appendix). Full results appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:generalization_full}.


\subsection{Discussion}
\label{sec:analysis}

EDCR validates at both cross-method ($\rho = -0.96$, $p < 0.001$) and per-relation ($\rho = -0.79$, $n = 76$, $p < 0.001$) levels, the latter orthogonal to Evi-F1 ($\rho = -0.06$); partial correlations are virtually unchanged when controlling for shared components (Appendix~\ref{sec:edcr_per_relation}). CED and flat-NLI produce comparable RSFT outcomes; stricter selection ($k{=}1$) outperforms $k{=}3$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:ablation_full}). RSFT reduces truncation, hallucination, and duplication errors by 6--22\% but does not improve evidence quality ($\Delta < 0.4$pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:error_analysis}): it improves \emph{what} the model extracts, not \emph{how well} it grounds extractions.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{fig_overview.pdf}
\vspace{-2mm}
\caption{Evidence-grounded DocRE produces relation triples with supporting sentence indices, unlike standard DocRE which outputs bare triples. RSFT selects the top-$k$ of $N$ CED-scored candidates per document for fine-tuning.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Task: Evidence-Grounded Document-Level Relation Extraction}
\label{sec:task}

Given a document $D = \{s_1, \ldots, s_n\}$ consisting of $n$ sentences and a set of entities $\mathcal{E}$ identified within $D$, the task of evidence-grounded document-level relation extraction (DocRE) requires predicting, for each entity pair $(e_h, e_t) \in \mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{E}$, a relation $r \in \mathcal{R} \cup \{\texttt{NA}\}$ together with a set of supporting evidence sentences $E \subseteq D$. Standard DocRE predicts only the triple $(e_h, r, e_t)$; the evidence-grounded formulation also requires $E$, which grounds each prediction in specific textual support (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}).

In our LLM-based approach, the model generates structured outputs that jointly encode both components. For each predicted relation, the output follows the format \texttt{(head, relation, tail) | Evidence: [S$i$][S$j$]}, requiring the model to produce complete evidence references inline with each triple rather than as a separate retrieval step. This design contrasts with encoder-based evidence methods such as SAIS~\citep{xiao2022sais} and DREEAM~\citep{ma2023dreeam}, which frame evidence identification as sentence-level binary classification. Because classification over a fixed sentence set does not increase output length, these methods incur no generation overhead. Our LLM formulation requires additional tokens for each evidence reference, creating a generation burden that scales with the number of predicted triples and their evidence sentences. This scale-dependent cost is the central phenomenon we investigate.


\subsection{Supervised Fine-Tuning Baseline}
\label{sec:sft}

We fine-tune all models with Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) \citep{hu2022lora} at rank $r{=}64$ and scaling factor $\alpha{=}128$, applied to all linear projections (attention and feed-forward). The training data consists of DocRED~\citep{yao2019docred} training documents converted to the structured output format described above, where each example maps a document and its entity set to the complete set of relation triples with evidence annotations. We also train a \emph{no-evidence} variant under identical configuration but with evidence references removed from the output format, producing only \texttt{(head, relation, tail)}. This variant serves as an upper bound on extraction performance by eliminating the generation cost of evidence, and allows direct measurement of the evidence tax at each model scale. Detailed hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size, number of epochs) are reported in the Appendix.


\subsection{Optimization Paradigms}
\label{sec:paradigms}

We evaluate three post-SFT optimization paradigms that differ in how they incorporate quality signals from the CED scoring function (\S\ref{sec:ced}).

\paragraph{GRPO.}
Group Relative Policy Optimization~\citep{shao2024deepseekmath} is an online RL method that eliminates the need for a separate value network by computing advantages within groups of sampled outputs. For each training document, the policy generates $G$ candidate responses, which are scored by CED. The advantage of each response is computed relative to the group mean, and the policy is updated to increase the probability of above-average outputs. We test two configurations: QLoRA \citep{dettmers2023qlora} with $G{=}4$ groups under 4-bit quantization, and bf16 full-precision LoRA with $G{=}8$. Because GRPO applies gradient updates that directly optimize the reward signal, evidence quality and extraction accuracy compete for gradient capacity. We analyze this multi-objective tension in Section~\ref{sec:spectrum}.

\paragraph{DPO.}
Direct Preference Optimization~\citep{rafailov2023direct} reformulates RLHF~\citep{ouyang2022instructgpt} as a classification problem over preference pairs, without explicit reward models. We construct preference data from the SFT model's sampled outputs: for each document, the response with the highest CED score is labeled as preferred and the lowest-scoring response as rejected. DPO then adjusts the policy to increase the log-probability ratio between preferred and rejected completions. We train for 1 and 3 epochs to observe the temporal dynamics of evidence-extraction trade-offs under implicit optimization.

\paragraph{RSFT.}
Rejection Sampling Fine-Tuning is a single-round variant of iterative self-improvement methods such as STaR~\citep{zelikman2022star}. The procedure is straightforward: for each SFT checkpoint, the corresponding model generates $N{=}8$ candidate outputs per training document, each candidate is scored by CED, and only the top-$k{=}1$ response is retained as training data. Each RSFT seed (s42, s43, s44) starts from its independently trained SFT checkpoint, preserving full pipeline variance. We use $k{=}1$ (best-of-$N$) selection throughout all experiments, selecting the single highest-scoring candidate per document. This yields approximately 3,000 training examples per model (one per document; exact counts vary slightly by model due to generation failures). Unlike GRPO and DPO, RSFT does not apply gradient-based optimization pressure toward a reward signal. Instead, it improves training data quality through filtering: the model trains on its own best outputs rather than all outputs. This distinction is critical because discrete selection over complete outputs cannot exploit the extraction-evidence trade-off that gradient methods find. The contribution of RSFT in our setting is not the framework itself, which is well-established, but its application to evidence-grounded DocRE with CED as a domain-specific quality filter.


\subsection{Contrastive Evidence Discrimination}
\label{sec:ced}

CED is a scoring function that evaluates the faithfulness of predicted evidence sentences to their associated relation triples. It builds on Natural Language Inference (NLI) to measure whether evidence actually entails the predicted relation, rather than merely co-occurring with the relevant entities.

For a predicted triple $(e_h, r, e_t)$ with evidence sentences $E$, CED constructs a positive NLI pair: the premise is the concatenation of sentences in $E$, and the hypothesis is a natural-language verbalization of the relation (e.g., ``$e_h$ has relation $r$ with $e_t$''). An NLI model based on DeBERTaV3 \citep{he2023debertav3} (\texttt{cross-encoder/nli-deberta-v3-base}) produces an entailment probability $p^+$ for this pair. The contrastive component introduces hard negatives drawn from the same document. For each triple, we collect sentences that mention $e_h$ or $e_t$ but are not in the predicted evidence set. These entity-sharing distractors serve as negative premises, and the NLI model scores each to obtain $p^-_1, \ldots, p^-_m$. The CED score for a single triple is:
\begin{equation}
\text{CED}(e_h, r, e_t, E) = p^+ - \max_j \, p^-_j
\label{eq:ced}
\end{equation}
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_ced_scoring.pdf}
\caption{CED scoring example. The positive pair (evidence $\to$ hypothesis) yields high entailment; the contrastive distractor (entity-sharing non-evidence) yields low entailment. CED measures the margin between them.}
\label{fig:ced_scoring}
\end{figure}
This formulation forces the scoring function to distinguish sentences that genuinely support the relation from sentences that merely mention the same entities. A high CED score indicates that the evidence provides relation-specific support beyond entity co-occurrence.

The document-level CED score aggregates over all predicted triples, weighted by extraction correctness: triples that match ground-truth relations contribute their CED scores, while incorrect triples receive zero credit. This coupling ensures that CED rewards both faithful evidence and accurate extraction jointly.

We also evaluate a \emph{flat-NLI} baseline that uses only the positive entailment score $p^+$ without the contrastive term. CED and flat-NLI produce comparable RSFT outcomes (mean Rel-F1 difference of 0.02pp across available seeds; Section~\ref{sec:analysis}), confirming that the contrastive formulation is not essential for data selection quality. We adopt CED as the default because its per-triple discrimination scores support interpretable error analysis.


\subsection{Evidence-Decoupling Ratio}
\label{sec:edcr}

For analysis, we define the Evidence-Decoupling Ratio (EDCR) as a diagnostic metric that quantifies the degree of misalignment between a model's extraction and evidence capabilities. EDCR is not a training component; it serves purely as an analytical tool for comparing optimization methods.

EDCR is defined as the distractor citation rate across all predicted triples:
\begin{equation}
\text{EDCR} = \frac{\sum_{i} |E_i^{\text{pred}} \setminus E_i^{\text{gold}}|}{\sum_{i} |E_i^{\text{pred}}|}
\label{eq:edcr}
\end{equation}
where the sum ranges over all predicted triples with at least one evidence citation. $E_i^{\text{pred}}$ is the set of evidence sentences cited for predicted triple $i$, and $E_i^{\text{gold}}$ is the corresponding gold evidence (empty for false-positive predictions). EDCR thus measures the fraction of all cited evidence that does not appear in the gold evidence for the associated triple.

A model with many false-positive triples will have high EDCR because all evidence for incorrect triples counts as distractor; even among correct triples, citing irrelevant sentences inflates the numerator. Conversely, accurate extraction with faithful evidence yields low EDCR. This dual sensitivity makes EDCR a natural diagnostic for extraction-evidence decoupling.

EDCR operates at two diagnostic levels. At the method level, comparing EDCR across seven unique method configurations reveals a negative correlation with Rel-F1 (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.96$, $n{=}7$, $p{<}0.001$). Because EDCR is mechanically influenced by false-positive triples (which share determinants with Rel-F1), this correlation should be interpreted as a diagnostic pattern rather than an independent discovery; its primary value lies in per-relation analysis. We validate EDCR's diagnostic utility through per-relation analysis (\S\ref{sec:analysis}), which avoids the shared-component concern present in cross-method correlation. At the per-relation level, computing EDCR for individual relation types reveals a robust negative correlation with Rel-F1 ($\rho{=}{-}0.79$, $n{=}76$, $p{<}0.001$) that is independent of Evi-F1 ($\rho{=}{-}0.06$, $p{=}0.58$). This independence means EDCR captures relation-specific extraction-evidence misalignment that aggregate evidence metrics miss. The SFT baseline EDCR (0.6797, three-seed mean) reflects the inherent distractor rate under standard training; EDCR values substantially exceeding this level (e.g., GRPO QLoRA at 0.9876) indicate RL-induced decoupling beyond what evidence production alone explains, while values below the baseline (e.g., RSFT at 0.6487) reflect improved co-optimization.
